  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass east of the New Jersey \nShore through tonight.\n\nLow pressure offshore continues to move north-northeast tonight\nand Monday. Heaviest rainfall remains offshore near the center\nof the low, currently located about 100 miles east of the\nJersey Shore, with very little rainfall now expected through the\nrest of tonight with diminishing winds. The onshore flow will \npromote extensive cloudiness across much, if not all, of our \narea tonight with some patchy fog possible too. Any lingering\nlight rain and drizzle tapers off overnight, and conditions \nwill dry out by Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong coastal storm is expected to bring \nimpactful weather to the region around Thanksgiving.  \n\nAfter a strong cold front moves through the region on Monday, a high \npressure system will be quick to follow in its wake, with much \ncooler conditions expected Tuesday and Wednesday. Highs look to \nmostly be in the 40s and 50s with overnight lows in the 20s to mid \n30s Tuesday night. \n\nBeginning on Wednesday, the next potential storm of concern will \nbegin to take shape off the Southeast coast. In the upper-levels, a \nnotable trough will be taking shape across the central US and then \nclose off into a strong upper-level low by Thursday near the Great \nLakes region before moving into the northeast on Friday. At the \nsurface, the low pressure system developing off the Southeast coast \nlooks to move towards the Mid-Atlantic Wednesday night and Thursday. \nThere remains model spread across the ensemble guidance but there \nappears to be a continued clustering near the coast or inland with \nthe low's track over the last few runs. There are still some \nsolutions in the ensembles showing more of an easterly offshore \ntrack. The exact track of the low pressure system will have big \nimplications of the impacts it brings to the area. \n\nAs our high pressure system retreats to the northeast for the end of \nthe week, strong warm air advection begins Wednesday into Thursday. \nWhat this means is the colder air will not stick around long. There \nis some potential for wintry precipitation at the onset of the \nprecipitation, mainly for the higher elevations in our NW zones. If \nthe precipitation is delayed from Wednesday night to be moreso after \ndaybreak Thursday, then the potential for wintry precipitation would \ntrend downward as well. This would be due to the warm air advection \ncontinuing to influence the thermal profiles more. For most of the \narea, this is looking to be a completely rain event. \n\nDue to still a range of solutions among guidance, there is uncertainty\naround the exact extent of the impacts. These impacts include heavy\nrain, strong winds, and coastal flooding. Details regarding specific\nimpacts will continue to come into better focus over the upcoming\ndays.",
